                "text": "**Analyzing Training Examples**\n\nI've started dissecting the training examples, beginning with a 5x7 grid. I'm focusing on the distribution of \"Color 3\" elements, specifically the vertical lines at columns 3 and 6. This initial analysis will establish a baseline for identifying patterns and relationships within the dataset.\n\n\n**Identifying Rectangles' Sides**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. Analyzing Example 1, it looks like vertical lines in the input define two parallel sides of a rectangle. The output then forms the horizontal sides connecting these vertical lines, excluding the corners. This seems to hold up! Now, checking the second example to test this theory further. I am on the right track!\n\n\n**Confirming Rectangle Logic**\n\nI've tested the rectangle hypothesis with Example 3, and it held! Now, horizontal sides act as the input, with the output constructing the vertical connections. This suggests a pattern: identify two parallel sides and generate the other two, discarding original sides. This looks like a complete and general rule for this pattern, now.\n\n\n",
